

[38;5;231m⏺[39m In-app + email, channel-agnostic. One consequence worth naming now: sending email can't happen inside a web request,
  so this needs a background worker regardless of what you pick next. That makes the following question cheaper than it
  looks.

  The fork is [1mcadence[0m, and it's really about trust. Immediate-per-change is the simplest to reason about, but a task
  edited five times in a minute sends five emails, and the fastest way to make a notification system worthless is to
  make people filter it out of their inbox. Batching collapses that into one message — and since a worker already has to
  exist for sending, batching costs a timestamp column and a query rather than new infrastructure.

  What batching costs you: latency. A change might sit up to the window length before the email goes out. In-app stays
  immediate either way, so anyone actually in the app sees it instantly; the window only delays the copy sent to people
  who aren't looking.

  Recommendation: in-app immediate, email batched per user on a short window, with a per-user daily-digest option for
  people who want less. Also in the spec regardless, as plain defaults: never notify you about your own changes, and
  collapse repeated edits to one task into a single entry.
